{
  "$comment": [
    "Tag catalogue for the store. This file is DATA, not code: adding, renaming, recolouring or",
    "re-mapping a tag only means editing this file. The app reads the copy embedded in the binary,",
    "and an optional override at %APPDATA%\\McServerLauncher\\store-tags.json wins over it, so a tag",
    "set can be changed or extended without rebuilding.",
    "",
    "How a project gets a tag. Every rule adds to a score and a tag applies from a score of 2:",
    "  categories  Modrinth category slugs (see https://api.modrinth.com/v2/tag/category). 3 points,",
    "              or 1.5 when the author ticked more than four topics (those authors tick everything).",
    "  keywords    WHOLE WORDS looked for in the title (3 points) and the short description (2 points,",
    "              plus 0.5 for each further keyword, up to 3). Lowercase and accent-insensitive;",
    "              punctuation separates words, so",
    "              a keyword may be a phrase (\"world generation\"). A trailing * accepts any ending:",
    "              \"optimiz*\" matches optimize, optimizes and optimization, \"map\" matches only map.",
    "              This is what keeps \"https\" from being \"tps\" and \"password\" from being \"sword\".",
    "  clientSide / serverSide  match the project's client_side / server_side value; they decide the",
    "              \"install\" tags and always apply.",
    "  fallback    true for a tag that is only worth showing when nothing better matched.",
    "  dominant    true for a tag that goes first when it applies and leaves room for one more topic.",
    "How a tag becomes a browse chip:",
    "  browse      true to show it as a filter chip in the browser.",
    "  facets      Modrinth facet groups used to filter (exact, preferred).",
    "  query       search text used when the tag has no facet of its own.",
    "  projectTypes  'mod' or 'plugin': the tag only applies to, and is only offered for, that kind",
    "              of server (default: both).",
    "priority breaks ties between tags with the same score and orders the chips (lower first)."
  ],
